---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { JURISDICTIONS } from "../lib/jurisdictions";

const title = "Jurisdiction matrix — property, title, gifts, and divorce risk";
const description =
  "Compare how selected jurisdictions treat sole title, separate property, homestead or matrimonial-home rules, marital agreements, gifts, and divorce-related transfer restrictions.";
---

<SiteLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Jurisdiction matrix: the same transfer can have a different answer in a different forum</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    “It is in my name” is never a complete legal analysis. Start with the governing forum, then classify
    the property, identify any homestead or matrimonial-home rules, check whether an agreement can change
    the classification, and determine whether a divorce case or injunction already limits transfers.
  </p>

  <p>
    This matrix deliberately does <strong>not</strong> demand one rule that works everywhere. It highlights
    the lawful tools each system actually provides, while marking the point where a transfer becomes
    vulnerable to joinder rules, equalization, community-property remedies, financial-relief powers,
    injunctions, or other statutory limits.
  </p>

  <div class="jurisdiction-grid">
    {JURISDICTIONS.map((forum) => (
      <article class="jurisdiction-card" id={forum.slug}>
        <h2>{forum.name}</h2>
        <p><strong>Property model:</strong> {forum.property_model}</p>
        <p><strong>Sole-title warning:</strong> {forum.sole_title_warning}</p>
        <p><strong>Lawful planning mechanisms:</strong> {forum.lawful_planning}</p>
        <p><strong>Danger zone:</strong> {forum.danger_zone}</p>
        <p class="fine"><strong>Last reviewed:</strong> {forum.reviewed}</p>
        <p class="fine">
          <strong>Primary sources:</strong>{" "}
          {forum.sources.map((source, index) => (
            <>
              {index > 0 && " · "}
              <a href={source.url} rel="noreferrer">{source.label}</a>
            </>
          ))}
        </p>
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>

  <section>
    <h2>A decision tree for a proposed gift or transfer</h2>
    <ol class="points">
      <li><strong>Which forum can affect the asset or the divorce?</strong> Residence, domicile, asset location, and the forum hearing the family case can all matter.</li>
      <li><strong>What is the asset legally classified as?</strong> Separate, community, marital, social, excluded, homestead, matrimonial home, or simply an asset subject to discretionary financial relief are different categories.</li>
      <li><strong>Does sole title matter here?</strong> Sometimes yes; often only partly; sometimes almost not at all.</li>
      <li><strong>Is there a spouse-joinder, consent, fiduciary, or registration rule?</strong> Houses receive special treatment in several jurisdictions represented here.</li>
      <li><strong>Has a divorce case, automatic restraint, injunction, or freezing order already attached?</strong> If so, a transfer that was lawful yesterday may require consent or court authorization today.</li>
      <li><strong>Is the transfer real?</strong> An outright gift means ownership actually leaves the donor. A side deal to return the asset later creates a materially different nominee or sham-transfer question.</li>
      <li><strong>What disclosure is required?</strong> A lawful transfer does not excuse a false financial affidavit, discovery response, disclosure schedule, or representation to a court.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why this is more useful than “never transfer assets before divorce”</h2>
    <p>
      Some legal systems give spouses broad power over genuinely separate property. Some permit spouses to
      convert or partition property by agreement. Some impose special restrictions only on a home. Others
      focus less on title and more on the court's ability to adjust property or reverse dispositions that
      defeat financial relief. The correct planning move therefore depends on the legal mechanism actually
      available in the governing forum.
    </p>
  </section>

  <p><a href="/divorce-horizon/">Apply the matrix to a divorce-on-the-horizon transfer.</a></p>
  <p><a href="/not-legal-advice/">Educational material only; verify current law and facts with independent counsel.</a></p>
</SiteLayout>
